ture/13931259/assets/65a6b5743ab9b6ef820744d0d6601b7dfd8de08a3f895661d96d2fe2cab8e0a7.png)\n\nLet ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/c9b75fa5f325825788ae2a64e227779c81af2454230ded498d7245dd9e0c66cf.png) be the endpoint of the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/7ab3eab6d396347c576f9565da06cf224deb593d51a3943062d6d96e56761ad4.png) Then\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/2159390e2c15cbecf0750f9c9b061c45b95bec745349cbd7c31e1aea834e8022.png)\n\nIn ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/934e201a8788fc6c8ff3a81d3946b86454ce5a69d0d9d1466cf0fff9a0de6ef5.png) we have:\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/597714ab8fa507e93f9e7ca11e7b91a8d752428df879dd5250647b9e54a87853.png)\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/7db8a41266cbcadf83361552751884e082648f2caf1ec786a372cb1e57a7e794.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/4c41b183428cf433289d7ef5bdadd3f83e100e5e4e0feb57d8c3afd647a05696.png) have opposite directions\n\nHence,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/ea63be325f82db32d019c101aff9fd96e6e153ffa86524fd755cb5b1bda8396e.png)\n\nSimilarly,\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/8388dbaf40f15bb799039154b6e2ed8f5ce8699b1e96ef47af03458d827dd392.png)\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/fd66eeaaabc163ea5004d6e89629ff6e28a310e555301703ab605c1dbd7f8ebb.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/e1d3b43e599ec0e4cb548b65a18315fb0d5fe155dad8b6f1fbabb63c42998c14.png) have opposite directions\n\nHence,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/874fc16afdc829b59ebb3f871e48722f6a11e475577e3195549d68cb2c836d30.png)\n\nAdditionally,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/033debb7dd8e9b9dbd5caa4fbe21557f26979dc19aa16ebd607fa29de34e9f5d.png)\n\nConsequently,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/1b1692325288b047f70aa0aa09921e576c3e61f22ee06b295bc72776a615b223.png)\n\nSince ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/cdd03b199d81550020678e938a618057ec249985289ab595de8701117ff08e67.png) the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/af7376fd2f2674cbb36c9a0128d1f90bbfc15082cb11fde35ce8b20a28b9043f.png) we get\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/f9b2860b8a585a97d625d1a0cad6d1373766af5260d731a173850f5d5c4bbc3c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096374 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/id #uuid "dccb5bac-e528-5b42-9949-36b3870fdde1" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-4287" :question/problem "Consider the picture below. Given that $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1106/Source/Images/bfa592bb1f4e5a292e1e94476ab6e5f1.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1106/Source/Images/49fbbe1f5dd4731054af33666b88cb64.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup ONM$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $ON=2 \\mid \\mathbf{n} \\mid$;\n- $\\overrightarrow{ON}$ and $\\mathbf{n}$ have the same direction\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{ON}=2\\mathbf{n}\n$$\n\n.\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $NM=2 \\mid \\mathbf{k} \\mid$\n- $\\overrightarrow{NM}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ have the same direction\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{NM}=2\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\n.\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= ON^(→) + NM^(→) \\\\\n&= 2n + 2k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}=2\\mathbf{n}+2\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\n.\n\nSince $\\mathbf{n} \\nparallel \\mathbf{k}$, the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{n}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda=2$ and $\\mu=2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096375 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "09d593cc-526e-5691-9a18-efff515eae59" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-4288" :question/problem "Consider the following diagram, where $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}$, $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf{b}$, and $X$ divides $AB$ in the ratio $3:1$. Find $\\overrightarrow{OX}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1106/Source/Images/8512f8ab1660315b7e5f0aa8beb302ff.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OX} = \\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AX}\n$$\n\nWe're told that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a}\n$$\n\nso we just need to work out $\\overrightarrow{AX}$.\n\nFirst, from $\\triangle OAB$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB^(→) &= AO^(→) + OB^(→) \\\\\n&=-OA^(→) + OB^(→) \\\\\n&=-a + b \\\\\n&= b - a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AX:XB=3:1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AX}=\\dfrac{3}{4}\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\dfrac{3}{4}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nOX^(→) &= OA^(→) + AX^(→) \\\\\n&= a + \\frac{3}{4}(b - a) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}a + \\frac{3}{4}b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096418 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"}] :question/id #uuid "839ed5f5-165f-5de5-aba1-551ac605475f" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-5212" :question/problem "Consider the picture below. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1106/Source/Images/c2827bf97f6d17572e9ac2b808d16a69.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1106/Source/Images/0b673cb49bd6ea0955e57952763b7458.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup OAC$, we have:\n\n- $AC=2 \\mid \\mathbf{b} \\mid$\n- $\\overrightarrow{AC}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ have the same direction\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AC}=2\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc &= OA^(→) + AC^(→) \\\\\n&= a + 2b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a} \\nparallel \\mathbf{b}$, the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{b}$, we get:\n$\\lambda=2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244911 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc7095eb-6985-5802-af0c-7428e098f774" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244913} {:answer/id #uuid "5022f32c-324b-5430-acb8-1b1c5bfc7fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244914} {:answer/id #uuid "817bc788-aa3f-5cce-a585-dd99a0106a6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244915} {:answer/id #uuid "f7699e4a-c740-55bf-968c-bde2e1328b2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244916} {:answer/id #uuid "24d6b208-43b8-5d3e-88a3-189b1a4b59f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24d6b208-43b8-5d3e-88a3-189b1a4b59f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244917} :answer-field/id #uuid "989889d2-e847-571d-b93a-933db73337d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a11bc4a2-fd7a-5333-a516-b057f55e0ba3" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-120254" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/6882323d2c8017e95100a0ab9d1091fdf149e884ef27d2d2bf59ab7609b1a960.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+2b,$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/a8309dc45ac43c6642010e744de5a062d6575d48ba85bd642df39dd261f47422.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=2|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-2a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2b-2a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+2b=-2a+2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244918 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc2ca1ac-baf7-50c5-9c60-57a1ed1533c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244920} {:answer/id #uuid "d4141f81-6004-5a81-ae43-db9641fafd45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244921} {:answer/id #uuid "55438839-19ba-5f55-be85-a1bbb8ca93ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244922} {:answer/id #uuid "18f316b1-e2ff-53ed-a4d7-e3aa849442f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244923} {:answer/id #uuid "0cc728fe-2da8-5566-a283-1e201b0de756" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244924}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc2ca1ac-baf7-50c5-9c60-57a1ed1533c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244920} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaab8ddf-1251-539a-9910-4a883dc533e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4d3394e1-e3ca-5fef-81fe-c269ab4905cd" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-120257" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/551316d1e36175ca302c5c472d4109a9bc9cadf32cc85d2a1751424a386070cc.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a-2b,$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a-2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $-2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/40a0137d6227212db9b7ef9f349029838f3f51398790ec5e1c304e12c3743a0f.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{1}{2}|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-2b-\\frac{1}{2}a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a-2b=-\\frac{1}{2}a-2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097082 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80120cea-42f6-536a-a12c-4c3b760859bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ = 1.5$ and $μ = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094899} {:answer/id #uuid "90ed0152-bc17-5c0a-9fac-769c5a688387" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ = 2$ and $μ = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094900} {:answer/id #uuid "b64b31a2-ea40-5bfb-917e-faf58d123194" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ =-2$ and $μ =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094901} {:answer/id #uuid "fe5f92d9-4714-55d4-8102-d106e968b4f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ =-2$ and $μ = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094902} {:answer/id #uuid "339e78f1-3955-5364-a7de-0d57fddd6b7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ = 2.5$ and $μ =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90ed0152-bc17-5c0a-9fac-769c5a688387" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ = 2$ and $μ = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094900} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9f338b7-0e6c-5006-af37-19877673bf78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1b7aee39-22ab-5444-970d-797a67e85565" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-19370" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1106/Source/Images/q-19370.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c = λa + μb$, find $λ$ and $μ$." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/601e049067de307ef2321ae9b516d1c7306d837ca0758c080074446a8c54389d.png)\n\nIn $△OAC$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OA=2|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OA}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=2a.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $AC=2|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=2b.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =2a+2b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b=2a+2b.$ \n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2db866fd-2588-5c42-982c-67d6a3b9a76b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244948} {:answer/id #uuid "3ba257bf-af61-5cb7-8b7e-d9463d5e5dcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2db866fd-2588-5c42-982c-67d6a3b9a76b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244948} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4756c5b-1750-559c-a816-407094d8be46" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d3fb92e3-522c-5f79-a155-8a768412d58e" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-321299" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/6fe3721ff3ab63e75be934c79b03d4ca601513c2e08b987a0bafd94c74856e90.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=a+\\lambda b$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $a.$ So, we begin with the vector $a,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/9c8d67718152bc1bc9569213369580b6fc6f5bc1fe0c63b637f14c9f659dfe27.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=\\frac{2}{3}|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=\\frac{2}{3}b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{2}{3}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+\\frac{2}{3}b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244950 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bfd1f22-b82c-5d60-8942-8eee6131112e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244952} {:answer/id #uuid "a77263ed-2e1b-5871-b785-5b889b9b4ec8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bfd1f22-b82c-5d60-8942-8eee6131112e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244952} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c13c6d7-7001-5808-b413-3e3a6b9553aa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244951} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de8e71e1-33e8-5960-8404-3b41af8a0fba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de8e71e1-33e8-5960-8404-3b41af8a0fba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244955} :answer-field/id #uuid "84e587f0-cf75-5361-9026-8c0f1f48cac4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a4338d8-16c5-555f-9994-d62335cb5f49" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-322576" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/36742531a5f9f5d77c5f7e90980120479f4142cecaf6e84937d76d938688409d.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=d,$ $\\overset{\\to }{ON}=e,$ and $T$ divides $\\overset{―}{MN}$ in the ratio $MT:TN=8:3.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OT}$ via the vectors $d$ and $e.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OT}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$d+$\n{{field-2}}\n$e$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MT}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=d,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{MT}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OMN,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{MN} & =\\overset{\\to }{MO}+\\overset{\\to }{ON} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{ON} \\\\  & =-d+e \\\\  & =e-d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $MT:TN=8:3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{MT}=\\frac{8}{11}\\overset{\\to }{MN}=\\frac{8}{11}(e-d).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OT} & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MT} \\\\  & =d+\\frac{8}{11}(e-d) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{11}d+\\frac{8}{11}e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244971 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f98f4480-07ec-5b85-b225-41142f1ac636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244973} {:answer/id #uuid "f96f8ec3-e71e-5a21-b32a-9bc87cb65f32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f96f8ec3-e71e-5a21-b32a-9bc87cb65f32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244974} :answer-field/id #uuid "01dc1a2d-47f1-56ae-aa52-c4a1cbbf5ea8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244972} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6b644b5-8659-5893-9dbc-fa8fd40213dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6b644b5-8659-5893-9dbc-fa8fd40213dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244976} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe422f1a-a05f-56be-a9b7-8220326b20e4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "963ee32e-9695-5de4-abd4-883ca842b97a" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-327075" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/e69402e97668c50f91258ca7e8dbb5206d1cd681e73bb9333303777adc866b70.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $i=\\lambda j+\\mu k$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/cb8209592ec6d5be435622999c1b3eb86e1756cbe9852be0efdbbf41d41777ff.png)\n\nIn $△OJL$ shown above, we have  \n\n- $OJ=2|j|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}$ and $j$ have the opposite direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=-2j.$  \n\nSimilarly:  \n\n- $JL=|k|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{JL}$ and $k$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{JL}=k.$  \n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\ni=\\overset{\\to }{OJ}+\\overset{\\to }{JL}=-2j+k.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $i=\\lambda j+\\mu k=-2j+k.$  \n\nSince $j∦k,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way.  Equating the coefficients, we get \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2,\\,\\mu =1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b618ba29-f2ef-5ebd-a698-937a01af1adc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244979} {:answer/id #uuid "f8112905-742b-5117-9113-54b51ab9f462" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244980} {:answer/id #uuid "e8422aa6-e138-5e37-8559-c6815c49b0ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244981} {:answer/id #uuid "5da4f64e-9b77-5a80-b4d0-555c64927105" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244982} {:answer/id #uuid "bc05fbf6-7334-5828-aeaf-9f8f78233303" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8422aa6-e138-5e37-8559-c6815c49b0ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244981} :answer-field/id #uuid "406a4f26-b5a6-54fa-a816-81d0fa9bf5ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89f49045-ce3b-55cb-aabe-852c9bd4db2d" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-367052" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/1d424b5db0534a8d9abc9b0aa84dcb2e6860a7ed51de7390fc4f7e91c1c33f2b.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+4b,$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+4b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $4b.$ So, we first draw the vector $4b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/d8fdfb800bd9bbb179d51115b86823d99029732fee9810281b9c587a32376993.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $4b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=4|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-4a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =4b-4a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+4b=-4a+4b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29336052-c4a9-582d-9220-3ee636684796" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=-\\frac{1}{3}v+\\frac{2}{3}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244993} {:answer/id #uuid "3daf1a81-0ba1-59df-8131-cdad3411a73b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{1}{3}u+\\frac{2}{3}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244994} {:answer/id #uuid "2d2fc91a-3744-579e-a0f6-950e4a878a70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{7}{9}u+\\frac{2}{9}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244995} {:answer/id #uuid "b54899df-6d8c-5fd5-a623-369f31e61ee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{2}{3}u+\\frac{1}{3}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244996} {:answer/id #uuid "4f35be66-b00c-5e55-b9b7-7a0b8eb76352" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{7}{9}v-\\frac{2}{9}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d2fc91a-3744-579e-a0f6-950e4a878a70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{7}{9}u+\\frac{2}{9}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244995} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee483764-dc6f-568b-bd11-a98dda2f857a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2af4a406-e134-5401-990f-61dd08996b3c" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-42669" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/040127594f639248736d4492f1853bcb1e6ef18c37db9242ebe466045713628a.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=u,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OV}=v$ and $W$ divides $\\overset{―}{UV}$ in the ratio $UW:WV=2:7.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OW}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=u,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{UW}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OUV,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{UV} & =\\overset{\\to }{UO}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-u+v \\\\  & =v-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $UW:WV=2:7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{UW}=\\frac{2}{9}\\overset{\\to }{UV}=\\frac{2}{9}(v-u).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OW} & =\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\  & =u+\\frac{2}{9}(v-u) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{9}u+\\frac{2}{9}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
